<h3 class="enhancer-section">How to participate</h3>

<p>
  The registration for the event is <b>now open!</b> See details at the <a href="/registration">registration page</a>.
</p>

<p>
  The event is open to all research IT specialists and research software engineers in Switzerland, whether your organization is part of the EnhanceR network or not.
  Participation is free of charge, but registration is mandatory. Lunch and coffee breaks for participants are sponsored by EnhanceR.
</p>
  
<p>You can also <a href="/assets/symposium_2026.ics">add the event to your calendar</a>.</p>

<h3 class="enhancer-section">Target audience</h3>

<p>
  The event's goal is to connect research IT specialists: experts who empower research by providing specific, advanced IT services.
</p>
<p>
  Examples include research software engineers, high-performance and scientific computing experts, data and information scientists,
  as well as research data management experts, data stewards and data analysis specialists in Swiss higher education institutions, Swiss research institutions
  and private companies that have a focus on serving public academic institutions (e.g. SWITCH).
</p>

<h3 class="enhancer-section">Keynote speakers</h3>

<p>
  <b>April Wang</b> is a Professor of Computer Science at ETH Zurich, where she directs the Programming, Education and Computer-Human Interaction Lab (PEACH Lab).
  Furthermore, she is a core faculty member at the Institute for Intelligent Interactive Systems, a member of ETH and Swiss HCI communities and an associated faculty member at the ETH AI Center.
</p>

<p>
  Her work covers the interface between computer systems and human users in the post-AI reality, including such topics as impacts of AI agents on software engineering,
  use of AI in computer science education and how to use the new tools to enhancer learning about deeper mathematical thinking.
  Beyond the Ai sphere Professor Wang’s group also works on the broader questions of human computer interactions in seeking ways to make programming more natural and intuitive.
  Hence, she and her group investigate creative ways to represent code, including not only ext, but also, shapes, animations, everyday objects, metaphors and more.
</p>

<p>
  <b>Cornelius Hempel</b> is the Group Head for Ion Trap Quantum Computing and deputy lab head (quantum) at the Paul Scherrer Institute,
  as well as an Advisory Board Member with the Open Quantum Institute. Holding a PhD from the University of Innsbruck,
  Dr. Hempel has also spent five years as a Research Fellow and Laboratory lead at the University of Sydney.
</p>

<p>
  His achievements to date include the first demonstration of a digital quantum simulation on a quantum computer,
  building the world’s first fully controllable 20 qubit system, or working with Harvard/Google colleagues on developing the variational quantum eigensolver.
  His work has been presented on the cover of Nature Chemistry in 2023.
</p>

